        "## MiniMax H3 Director\n\nThe LTX Director timeline, wired to **MiniMax H3**.\n\n### Two model paths\nThe dropdown next to **Delete** on the toolbar picks the conditioning path, and each path needs its **own UNet**:\n\n| Toolbar | UNet to load |\n|---|---|\n| `Refs OFF (fl2va)` | `minimax_h3_fl2va_*` |\n| `Refs ON (ref2va)` | `minimax_h3_ref2va_*` |\n\nThis workflow ships with **fl2va** loaded (text to video + optional first/last frame).\n\n### How the timeline maps\n- **Text/prompt segments** compile into a storyboard prompt: `[0s-1.5s] shot one…`. That is H3's native notation for timed control.\n- **An image at the start** of the window becomes `first_frame`; an image marked as an **end frame** (or one that runs to the end) becomes `last_frame`. H3 anchors keyframes only at those two positions.\n- **Images in the middle**, the **reference-video track** and the **audio track** need `Refs ON (ref2va)`; they become `<Picture i>`, `<Video k>` and `<Audio j>`.\n- **Character slots** `@char1..@char3` become `<Picture i>` in ref2va, or their VLM description in fl2va.\n\n### Length and canvas\nLength snaps up to H3's **17k+5 frame grid at 24 fps** — 5 s becomes 124 frames (5.17 s). The native canvas is a 768 px short edge capped at 768×1344.\n\n### Audio\nH3 generates stereo audio **jointly** with the video, so there is nothing to inpaint. The sampler's single latent feeds both decoders. If you want your own music instead, wire the Director's `combined_audio` into `CreateVideo`.\n\n### Preview\n**MiniMax H3 Preview Override** sits between the Director and the sampler. ComfyUI's built-in preview only ever draws the *first* latent frame — this streams the whole shot while it denoises, so you see cuts, motion and timing from the first steps. Needs ComfyUI-VideoHelperSuite for the player. Bypass it (Ctrl+B) if you want the plain single-frame preview back.\n\n### Prompt output\nThe `prompt` output returns the exact storyboard text that was encoded — connect a preview node to it when a shot is not landing."
      ],
      "title": "Note: How this works",
      "color": "#222",
      "bgcolor": "#000"
    },
